# Engineering Documentation

Make documentation precise, retrievable, current, and independently
verifiable. Prefer one authoritative document with links over duplicated truth.

## Writing

- Lead with purpose and operational consequence; use direct, specific language.
- Define acronyms on first use and use terms exactly as `docs/GLOSSARY.md` defines them.
- Use short sections, descriptive headings, relative links, and tables only when they improve comparison.
- State units, assumptions, limits, owners, status, dates, and evidence where relevant.
- Distinguish fact, requirement, decision, plan, and example. Do not present an aspiration as implemented behavior.
- Avoid unsupported claims such as “production-ready,” “compliant,” “real-time,” or “enterprise-grade.”

## Document Types

- API documentation defines contracts, types, units, preconditions, errors, examples, and versioning behavior.
- Architecture documentation shows boundaries, dependency direction, data flow, runtime sequence, constraints, and trade-offs.
- ADRs are append-only and record context, decision, rejected alternatives, consequences, and supersession links.
- READMEs answer purpose, layout, allowed contents, usage, and local rules; keep commands executable.
- PRDs own product requirements; the roadmap owns sequencing; progress owns current state; the changelog owns history.

## Maintenance

- Update affected documentation and Cursor rules in the same change as behavior.
- Verify links, anchors, phase numbers, requirement IDs, and cross-document claims after edits.
- Never reproduce controlled AIAG or SEMI content; cite conceptual alignment and avoid conformance claims.
- Preserve historical decisions. Mark superseded content and link its replacement rather than rewriting history.
